{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789040089,"nonce":"IGqzFn5fLIE4wJm5Sw104rJThwk=","title":"The Windows JIT loses the program at its first throw","message":"`test` / **aot executable (windows-2022)** fails because the JIT half of the comparison is broken, not the AOT half. That is a change of subject from what this job used to mean, so the old reading in windows-ci-aot-failure is stale.\n\nWhat the AOT binary does now, after 909d581 gave Win64's `_setjmp` the frame argument it is spelled with:\n\n    test_stdlib          aot: 63 lines, exited ok=true      \u003c- runs to completion\n    test_safe_cast_virtual  aot: prints 'cast failed as expected'\n    test_std_reflect_type   ok\n\nWhat the JIT does on the same programs:\n\n    test_stdlib          jit: 38 lines, exited ok=false, last line '--- exceptions ---'\n    test_safe_cast_virtual  jit: prints nothing\n\nBoth stop exactly where a throw has to be caught.\n\nWhy this was invisible before: BOTH halves died at the same place, so aot_smoke compared two identically truncated outputs and called them equal. test_stdlib was reported ok for that reason. Repairing one half is what made the other visible -- the comparison only ever asserted that the two agree.\n\nRuled out: the frame argument is not what breaks the JIT. Emitting it on both paths and emitting it only on the AOT path produce byte-identical JIT output (38 lines, ok=false, same last line), so the JIT's failure is upstream of the setjmp call shape. Do not re-run that experiment.\n\nWhere to look: the JIT resolves `_setjmp` to the address of the runtime's own binding (`crate::hl::_setjmp`, the non-AOT arm of `setjmp_ptr`) rather than declaring a symbol for a linker to bind, and its throw path calls `hl::longjmp` under `#[cfg(windows)]` (std/src/error.rs). One of those two is not what Win64's CRT expects.\n\nNo Windows machine here, so CI is the only oracle; each attempt costs a push and ~12 minutes.","files":null}]}